WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.180
Something tells me there may have been some Film Theory fans working on the set of the new Sonic movie.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:07.280
Remember in the trailer, when they showed Sonic running past a radar gun,

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.820
And it returned a value of 760 miles per hour?

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:14.480
Well, that trailer clip prompted me to respond with this video,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.300
where I looked at the reality of radar guns.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:19.980
Coming to the conclusion that no gun would be able to reach a speed that high,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.140
and that the radar would instead cap out around 200 miles per hour.

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:27.380
And wouldn't you know it, the scene was changed in the final movie.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.380
Where Sonic's new speed clocks in around 250-300 miles per hour.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:33.200
Coincidence?

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.300
Maybe, but then how do you explain the scene

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.980
when Robotnik, out of the blue, just encourages Sonic to slow down,

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.840
telling him that his speed is dangerous for his joints.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:45.060
"They proved it, you know..." is exactly what he says.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:47.360
Now, who is this "they", Eggman?

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.720
Could it be US?

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.360
I mean, we did dedicate an entire episode to whether Sonic's body could survive his own extreme speeds,

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.160
concluding that it's ultimately bad for his joints.

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:59.800
I'm just saying, there were more than a few parallels to our past Sonic episodes.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:01.420
And I'm not mad, by any means.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.860
In fact, I'm kind of flattered.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:05.600
But, next time, you could at least invite me to the premiere.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:06.720
Now, if you'll excuse me,

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.560
I'm off to feed my sadness with this never-ending pasta bowl from Olive Garden.

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:15.880
*slurps*

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:17.260
It never ends!

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.180
Hello, Internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:23.620
Where I'm here to remind you,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.440
Sonic turned his life around in one year, you can too.

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:30.180
Today, I'd like to start by congratulating the team working on the Sonic movie.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:31.100
Well done, guys!

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.940
You managed to do the impossible,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.960
like, 3 times over, by my calculations.

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.320
In one movie you managed to

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.220
1. Create a good video game movie,

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.640
2. Take the criticism of the internet rage machine and use it to better your film,

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:47.180
and 3. Make a kids movie with a minimal amount of cringe.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:50.240
I mean, having Sonic floss, not just once, but twice

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.500
was starting to push your luck a bit there,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.180
but, you know what? I'll give you the benefit of the doubt.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.420
I like to think of it as a callback to the days when Sonic

00:01:56.420 --> 00:01:59.060
had to maintain his weird, little human-like teeth.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.380
I mean, why floss when you can ~floss~?

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:03.300
But, seriously, I had a lot of fun with this movie.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.900
It was a perfect balance of original story telling and fan service.

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.760
The subtle references to Chaos Emeralds,

00:02:08.760 --> 00:02:11.220
the Sega Saturn, the theme music from the game.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.920
Some of Sonic's iconic poses,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:17.420
the fact that his punch is about as effective as being slapped across the face with a flaccid noodle.

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:20.960
I mean, you even man aged to keep some of the big cameo reveals secret,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:23.260
including, what was, to me, the highlight of the movie,

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:24.820
the guest appearance of Sanic.

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:27.960
I mean, how'd you pull that one out of your egg sac, Eggman?

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.740
But, don't let that comedically oversized head get any bigger there, Sonic Team.

00:02:31.740 --> 00:02:33.540
Because, today can't be all about praise.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.060
I got to knock you for some things.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.300
First off, blatant product placement for Zillow?

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.140
C'mon! We all know that Trulia's where it's at, fam!

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:42.160
What, what!

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:46.520
Actually, to be fair, any smart apartment shopper would be wise to use all the major resources available to them,

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.260
so, I'm not really taking sides.

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:49.540
That was just for comedic effect.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.500
Then there's Longclaw the owl.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.140
Let me get this straight:

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:55.380
You made up a new character for this movie?

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:59.500
This is a franchise where there's more throwaway characters than Game of Thrones.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.920
And you just added to the pile instead of taking an established one?

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:06.640
I mean, I guess it follows in the great tradition of the fandom's OCs.

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.680
The only thing missing with Longclaw was a watermark over her head reading,

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.980
"DO NOT STEAL," in all caps.

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.720
But it's the last eency-weency nitpick I have that I want to focus on for today's episode.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:20.160
You know, a small, teeny, tiny little problem I have

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:22.700
with the entire premise of the movie!

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:24.680
Sonic drops his rings through a magic portal,

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.560
and they end up in San Francisco.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.220
So he and his human buddy Tom need to get them back

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.040
via a road trip.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:34.660
You took the video game character known for running real fast,

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.960
and you put him in a truck for a road trip.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.680
Going, like, 65 miles an hour.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:41.220
Oh, sure.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.400
You make the excuse that Sonic can't find San Francisco, and, yeah,

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.980
I get that the entire road trip was a veiled excuse for Sonic to connect with humans

00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:48.940
in a way that he always longed to,

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:51.220
but was unable to because of his self-imposed isolation,

00:03:51.220 --> 00:03:52.080
I get it!

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:54.620
But I just don't care.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:59.580
As I crashed into the cold, dark water of the Pacific, I realized a few things:

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:01.160
I have no idea where I'm going.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:04.560
And clearly, I'm not going to be able to do this on my own.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.220
Don't sell yourself too short, there, Sonic.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:10.700
I have a pretty good feeling that you would have been just fine left on your own.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:14.380
So, today, we're putting that movie's premise to the test.

00:04:14.380 --> 00:04:18.320
Was Sonic's road trip with Tom a faster way of them getting to San Francisco

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.440
than Sonic just running there by himself?

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:22.220
Knowing what we know about Sonic's speed,

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:25.240
as well as his apparent inability to use a basic map,

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:27.720
would Sonic blindly running across the country,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.400
ultimately get him to San Francisco faster than hopping in the ol' Chevy Silverado

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.980
and driving there human-style?

00:04:33.980 --> 00:04:35.780
So, let's start off with what we know.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:37.280
In the movie, their entire road trip

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.320
takes roughly 2 days.

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:40.080
First night is Sonic's big night out,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.020
eating chili dogs at the local biker bar,

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.860
by the late afternoon of the next day, they're in

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.020
San Francisco eating clam chowder and fighting off Robotnik.

00:04:47.020 --> 00:04:49.020
But, I want to double check that figure.

00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:51.180
Seems like it's a bit too short.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:53.880
We know that their journey begins in Green Hills, Montana,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.480
a fictional city, that, apparently,

00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:58.820
isn't all that far off from the World's Largest Rubberband Ball.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:02.600
A clue that you think might help us identify the real-world location of Green Hills,

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:03.440
but it doesn't.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.720
The largest rubberband ball in the world was made in

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:08.500
Lauderhill, Florida, and now lives in a museum.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:11.220
Just a couple of states away from Montana.

00:05:11.220 --> 00:05:15.420
I mean, Sonic could be seeing a sign for the world's second largest rubberband ball,

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:20.360
a 175,000 band behemoth that lives in Eugene, Oregon,

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:23.700
but it's still too early in the road trip for them to be all the way over here,

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:25.120
when they started somewhere in Montana.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.020
So, you know what?

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:29.220
Maybe we're better locating Green Hills using another method.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.080
According to the welcome sign for Green Hills,

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:33.860
the city has a population of 1,981,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.080
and an elevation of 3,445 feet.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:43.260
Now, finding a place in Western Montana that fits both of those criteria is surprisingly challenging.

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:46.560
Because the Rocky Mountains are going right through that part of the country,

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:48.960
elevations tend to be higher, on average, there.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.620
4,500 feet and up.

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:55.140
But by cross referencing Montana elevation maps with Montana population maps,

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:57.640
I was able to roughly pick out Powell County.

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:00.320
And specifically the city of Deer Lodge

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:04.200
as our prime candidate for the real-world equivalent of Green Hills.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:06.660
With an elevation of 4,567 [feet],

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:09.020
and a population of just under 3,000

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:12.100
-- yeah, it isn't exact, but it's pretty darn close.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.920
And about as close as you're going to find

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:15.220
in this part of Montana.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:19.740
What also helped me settle on Deer Lodge was this moment in the movie, when Robotnik

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.480
is tracking Tom and Sonic down.

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:23.540
We see red dots marked out on the map,

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:26.180
presumably key areas that they visited along the trip.

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:28.660
And the starting dot is roughly here,

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:30.980
right where Idaho has that first little chunk in the state.

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:33.320
So, track that over, and BOOM!

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:35.200
We're back in Powell County.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:37.920
So, citizens of Deer Lodge, all 3,000 of you

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.660
who are clearly watching this show,

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:42.020
no longer will your city just be known as

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:44.960
the home for the Montana State Prison...

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:46.340
Really? Jeez.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.000
That is a bummer of a thing to be known for.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.120
Well, all of you watching from your little prison TVs

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.860
while you're stamping out license plates,

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:55.020
take pride knowing that you are now the featured city

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.480
of a pretty okay video game movie!

00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:00.220
Anyway, using Google Maps to calculate the length

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.880
of the road trip from Deer Lodge to San Francisco,

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.640
we see that there are two possible routes.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:08.060
The Northern route, going up through Washington and down through Oregon,

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.860
and another, more direct route headed down through Idaho and Nevada.

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.740
Again, going back Robotnik's map that

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:17.880
I mentioned earlier, it seems like Tom took the Northern and longer route.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:20.860
We see red dots in both of those Northwest states.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:23.180
Plus, when Robotnik zooms in on the map,

00:07:23.180 --> 00:07:25.480
he's looking at Southern Oregon, which, again,

00:07:25.480 --> 00:07:27.940
isn't anywhere close to the southern route.

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.160
So, knowing this, we see that it's an 18 ½ hour

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.740
road trip from Deer Lodge to San Francisco.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:36.860
Pretty aggressive for a 2 day road trip, but it's certainly not impossible.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.420
It's actually a lot faster than I expected.

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:40.780
So, now the question is:

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:46.960
Would Sonic have been able to blindly find San Francisco in that same 18 ½  hour time?

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:49.460
Or, was the road trip, unbelievably,

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.800
a more efficient way of getting him to his final goal?

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.260
We know for a fact that Sonic can read.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:57.060
In the opening montage of his life on Earth,

00:07:57.140 --> 00:08:00.260
we see him reading stacks of Flash comic books.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.140
He also knows that his rings wound up in San Francisco

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.060
because he read it on Tom's skin-tight tee-shirt.

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.020
So, it's less of an issue of Sonic not knowing where San Francisco is when he sees it,

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.240
but more of him just needing help finding the city outright in the first place.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.820
Now, I'm going to assume that he isn't reading road signs to follow directions,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:18.980
or mile markers.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.520
We're just going to assume that, once he reaches the city,

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:23.680
if he manages to reach San Francisco,

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:27.080
he'll be able to visually see the Transamerica building where his rings landed,

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.780
because it's just such a recognizable piece of architecture.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:31.920
So, now, how do we calculate the odds

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:34.320
of him finding San Francisco in the first place?

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:36.080
Well, I propose that we do it by

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.780
working under the assumption that he's just going to

00:08:37.780 --> 00:08:40.380
run every street in America until he finds it.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:42.320
Just a bunch of trial and error.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:43.900
Brute force his way around the country

00:08:43.900 --> 00:08:45.860
until he finally sees a sign that says,

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:49.820
"Welcome to San Francisco," or, even better, just winds up at the foot of the tower.

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:52.380
Which, of course, as any Sonic episode does,

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:56.100
leads us to the inevitable question of Sonic's speed.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:57.780
Now, last time we covered the Sonic movie,

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:01.000
we calculated that Sonic was running near the speed of light.

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:04.420
It's the only way he'd be able to emit an electromagnetic discharge

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.620
capable of shutting off power grids and interrupting satellite flight.

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.780
But, let's not go to that extreme of a speed just yet.

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.640
Let's give the movie the best chance it has at winning this little road race.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:15.440
Right at the start of their adventure,

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.740
shortly after Tom and Sonic escape Green Hills,

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:21.660
Tom kicks Sonic out of the truck, telling him to find San Francisco on his own,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.680
prompting the scene that I played earlier.

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.280
Sonic runs west, just like Tom tells him,

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:28.120
and he crashes into the Pacific Ocean,

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:29.920
and then runs back with a fish on his head.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:31.500
Now, timing this scene out,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.740
-- yes, I actually had a stopwatch in the theatre,

00:09:33.740 --> 00:09:36.500
so not only was I the weird, single guy in a room full of

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:37.900
families and small children,

00:09:37.900 --> 00:09:40.380
but I was also the weird single guy in a room full of

00:09:40.380 --> 00:09:42.780
families and small children carrying a stopwatch

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:44.760
and using it during the movie

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.300
-- but from the moment he takes off to the moment

00:09:46.300 --> 00:09:49.740
he returns to the truck, it takes exactly 3.9 seconds.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:51.380
I'm just going to round this to 4, just to

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.000
make the calculations a bit more simple.

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:54.640
Speaking of 4 seconds, I have a challenge

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:55.680
where you gotta go fast:

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:56.380
can you,

00:09:56.380 --> 00:09:59.040
in the time that it took Sonic to run from Green Hills

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:00.320
to the Pacific Ocean and back,

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:02.260
ring the subscription bell, and leave a comment

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:03.320
down below?

00:10:03.320 --> 00:10:03.840
Bet you can't!

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:05.160
Let's test it out, shall we?

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.065
Four!

00:10:06.065 --> 00:10:07.080
Three!

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:07.840
Two!

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:09.020
One!

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:09.960
Did you do it?

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.180
Well, whether you did or didn't, just know that your subscription is appreciated.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:14.760
I'm trying to get to 10 million subscribers

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:16.200
sometime this year on this channel,

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.540
and I need all the help I can get,

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.720
so, you subscribing means a lot.

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.000
Thank you so much!

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:21.880
Now on with the episode.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.380
If he took Tom's advice literally,

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:25.780
Sonic ran straight west to the Pacific,

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:27.540
and then back in that amount of time.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.560
drawing a straight line out from Deer Lodge

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:31.420
out the Pacific, you see that, at this point,

00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.240
there are about 550 miles,

00:10:33.400 --> 00:10:35.660
or 885 kilometers away from the ocean.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.360
That means, in 4 seconds, Sonic traveled

00:10:38.500 --> 00:10:43.600
1,100 miles, or 1,770 kilometers.

00:10:43.780 --> 00:10:47.200
That's 275 miles, or 442 kilometers a second,

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:52.580
or, our final number of 990,000 miles per hour.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:56.200
1.6 million kilometers an hour!.

00:10:56.200 --> 00:10:59.240
Now, Tom gives Sonic the instructions of, "head west,"

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:00.400
so, let's do that.

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.780
Let's assume that Sonic explores every roadway west of

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:06.120
where he currently is in Green Hills, Montana.

00:11:06.120 --> 00:11:08.180
That's mostly going to be the states of Idaho

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:11.580
Washington, Oregon, California, a bit more of Montana,

00:11:11.640 --> 00:11:12.740
and Nevada.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:14.920
So, I added up all of the roadways,

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:17.900
both paved and unpaved, for all of those states,

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:19.760
and found that we're dealing with about

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:23.540
1,083,000 miles worth of roadway.

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:25.940
1.7 million kilometers.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.220
Sonic would be able to cover all of that

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.600
in a little over an hour.

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:34.140
But, okay, maybe Sonic doesn't know what direction west is.

00:11:34.140 --> 00:11:35.280
Maybe he goes the wrong way.

00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:37.940
Well, across all 50 states, the US has

00:11:37.940 --> 00:11:41.700
4,092,729 miles of roadway.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.240
6.58 million kilometers.

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:47.900
It is far and away the best, longest, most robust

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:49.380
road system in the world.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:52.720
And Sonic would be able to cover it all in 4 hours.

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:54.920
Giving him more than enough time to get lost,

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:56.380
double back, and stop for every

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.020
giant rubberband ball that he sees along the way

00:11:59.020 --> 00:12:01.500
while still making it to the Transamerica building

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:04.200
3 times over before Tom's truck would.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:05.760
And remember, that's still me assuming

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:07.280
the worst-case-scenario.

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:09.560
That's Sonic not traveling at light speed,

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.060
not reading road signs, that's Sonic traveling

00:12:13.060 --> 00:12:14.540
all the roads in America before finally stumbling

00:12:14.540 --> 00:12:17.800
across San Francisco as his very last destination,

00:12:17.800 --> 00:12:20.480
'cause, you know, one of the nation's largest cities

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:21.900
might be hard to miss.

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:24.140
But you know what? Call me Angelica Schuyler

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.820
'cause I am still not satisfied!

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.380
What if Sonic got, really, REALLY lost,

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.460
and took a roadway that went south of the border?

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:31.880
Or up to Canada?

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.540
Or maybe he just wanted to enjoy a nice huevos rancheros near the Nazca lines.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:39.260
So I tallied up all the roadways in all the countries and

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:42.020
territories of both North and South America,

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:43.200
because I didn't feel like separating

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:44.900
out the island nations, to be honest.

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:47.500
So I went the whole nine yards, and I got myself

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:51.220
7,029,306 miles of roadway,

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:54.140
11. 3 million kilometers.

00:12:54.160 --> 00:12:57.940
7 hours and 6 minutes worth of Sonic runntime

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:00.760
to run every street, every roadway

00:13:00.760 --> 00:13:02.300
both paved and unpaved,

00:13:02.300 --> 00:13:05.780
in every piece of land in all of North and South America,

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:06.520
and still,

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:09.880
STILL he is taking up less than half the time

00:13:09.880 --> 00:13:11.640
it would require for Tom to drive him

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:13.600
that 18 ½ road trip.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:15.020
And so, yeah. I went there,

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:16.380
because I'd already come this far,

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:18.100
why not go the final step?

00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:21.100
I went to the CIA's World Factbook and added up

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:24.240
all 215 entries they had for the roadways

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:25.600
around the world.

00:13:25.600 --> 00:13:28.040
Paying attention to every last number,

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:33.140
even Tuvalu's little reported 8 kilometers of paved roadway because that's how type A I am!

00:13:33.140 --> 00:13:36.420
An absolutely tedious process of data crunching

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:38.980
that was rendered entirely useless when I got to the

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:41.300
final 4 entries of that CIA list,

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:45.440
and saw that "World" was a separate entry unto itself.

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:49.060
Why wouldn't you put that at the top of the list, CIA?!

00:13:49.220 --> 00:13:51.780
Or, at the very least, the very bottom?

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:53.360
I know that you watch this show,

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:57.060
because you're nervous about the things that are in my search history from researching this series,

00:13:57.060 --> 00:13:58.640
so c'mon guys!

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:02.660
Do some basic data entry best practices for me, alright?

00:14:02.660 --> 00:14:05.480
Anyway, I came up with, well, I came up with

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:09.040
a little more than half of what they reported as the world total.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.980
Not 100% sure how it happened, to be honest.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:13.540
I think they counted the European Union twice

00:14:13.540 --> 00:14:14.360
in their calculations,

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:16.520
because their numbers just don't make sense

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:17.940
relative to what they're reporting.

00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.380
Central Intelligence Agency my foot!

00:14:20.380 --> 00:14:22.380
More like Crappy Information Archive.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.780
And that, my friends, is why it pays to be thorough

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.720
and double check government organizations

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:28.540
with your own Excel spreadsheet.

00:14:28.540 --> 00:14:32.820
Regardless, my final total for all the roadways in the world,

00:14:32.820 --> 00:14:36.320
was 25,010,432 miles,

00:14:36.320 --> 00:14:39.140
40.25 million kilometers.

00:14:39.240 --> 00:14:43.600
A distance that would take Sonic 1 day and 1 hour to run.

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:45.380
Meaning that, finally, FINALLY,

00:14:45.380 --> 00:14:47.920
we've reached a number that Tom's road trip is able to beat.

00:14:47.980 --> 00:14:48.780
Or is it?

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:53.400
Remember, we said that 18 ½ hours was Tom's trip if he drove non-stop,

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.000
but we know that they took breaks,

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:56.440
they beat up biker thugs,

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:59.040
they farted around in their hotel room, figuratively and literally.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:00.020
(flatulence)

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:03.280
In total, that road trip was at least a day and a half,

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:06.880
probably 36-40 hours total before everything is said and done.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:10.080
Meaning that Sonic, running every roadway in the world,

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.780
would still end up taking a shorter time than

00:15:12.940 --> 00:15:16.540
sitting shotgun next to Tom down the ol' Route 90 West.

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:20.740
And again, remember, this is me taking the conservative estimate on every step along the way, here.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.260
We're not even factoring in his ability to travel near the speed of light.

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.420
And don't you get started on me about how they needed Tom

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.980
to smuggle him to the top of the building when he got there.

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:32.580
At the start of the road trip, they didn't know that little bit of information.

00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:34.580
And, as Sonic and Eggman chase each other

00:15:34.580 --> 00:15:36.500
through the city in the opening sequence of the movie,

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:42.120
we see Sonic's ability to literally run up the exterior walls of buildings.

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:45.300
Sure, it's an external view, very wideout from the action,

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.220
but still, he is scaling the building like a champ.

00:15:48.220 --> 00:15:51.560
It's almost as if the whole thing was manufactured

00:15:51.560 --> 00:15:54.480
by Sonic to make Tom feel important in the first place.

00:15:54.480 --> 00:15:56.600
That manipulative little hedgehog.

00:15:56.660 --> 00:15:59.780
Seems like speed isn't his only super ability.

00:15:59.780 --> 00:16:01.880
So there you have it, my little chaotics.

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.820
You should watch the Sonic movie for a lot of reasons.

00:16:03.820 --> 00:16:06.160
It's fun, it's funny, it does the characters justice.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:09.500
Just don't roll in expecting a premise that makes a whole lot of sense.

00:16:09.500 --> 00:16:12.500
Or an accurate location of the world's largest rubberband ball.

00:16:12.500 --> 00:16:14.800
OR an appearance from Charmy the Bee.

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:17.020
C'MON GUYS! You make Longclaw,

00:16:17.180 --> 00:16:19.340
but you neglect Charmy the Bee??

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:22.660
Time to fire up the internet rage machine one more time for the sequel!

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:24.300
But first, I have to shop for an apartment.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.840
Zillow ... dot com.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:29.800
But hey! That's just a theory!

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:31.680
A Film Theory!

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:33.040
Aaaaand cut.

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:43.700
*video game music*

